<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brihadratha Maurya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 185 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brihadratha Maurya was the last emperor of the Maurya dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ruling a much-reduced realm from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> in the early 2nd century BCE. Around 185 BCE his commander-in-chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a>, assassinated him while he reviewed the army and seized the throne, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga dynasty</a> and ending Maurya rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brihadratha-maurya.jpg" alt="Brihadratha Maurya" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lomas_Rishi_entrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Brihadratha Maurya was the ninth and last emperor of the Mauryan Empire. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> he succeeded his father Shatadhanvan. His territories were centred on the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and had shrunk considerably since the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>.</p><p>Before his death the northwest was invaded. Between 190 and 186 BCE the Greco-Bactrian king Demetrius, also called Dharmamita, entered the region, which covers parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and occupied it. The Mauryans had diplomatic alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and the Greco-Bactrians may have regarded them as allies. The Sri Lankan Paramparapustaka chronicle states that Brihadratha married Demetrius' daughter Berenice, called Suvarnnaksi in Pali. Demetrius established his rule in the Kabul Valley and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, but soon had to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to fight a fierce battle, probably against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a>.</p><p>A further Greek invasion reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> is hypothesised from the Yuga Purana, which describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a>, approaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saketa">Saketa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and reaching the city, where its mud walls are cast down and all the realms fall into disorder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brihadratha was killed by his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a>, who took the throne and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harshacharita">Harshacharita</a> of Bāṇabhaṭṭa says that Pushyamitra paraded the entire Mauryan army before the emperor on the pretext of showing him its strength, and then killed his master in front of the assembled troops. His death ended the Maurya dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brihadratha Maurya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE the last Maurya emperor, <b>Brihadratha</b>, was assassinated by his commander-in-chief, Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded the Shunga dynasty in the Gangetic heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE Pushyamitra Shunga, commander of the Maurya army, assassinated the last Maurya emperor, <b>Brihadratha</b>, during a military parade and took the throne at Pataliputra.</blockquote><a href="/state/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a></li></ul></section>
